The merge bullets match `mergePlugConfigs` in `src/Utils/plugConfig.ts`. They are **right**.

## What is being merged

Two lists, keyed by **slug**:

1. **API** — `GET /api/v1/plug_config/` (`enabledPlugins.configs`)
2. **Build-time** — `REACT_ENABLED_APPS` (`careConfig.careApps`) plus in-tree `localDevPluginConfigs`

```38:63:/home/yaswanth/ohc/care_fe/src/Utils/plugConfig.ts
export const mergePlugConfigs = (
  apiConfigs: PlugConfig[] = [],
): ResolvedPlugConfig[] => {
  const configs = new Map<string, ResolvedPlugConfig>();

  for (const config of apiConfigs) {
    configs.set(config.slug, {
      ...config,
      source: "api",
      isReadOnly: false,
    });
  }

  for (const config of getBuildTimePlugConfigs()) {
    const existing = configs.get(config.slug);

    configs.set(config.slug, {
      slug: config.slug,
      meta: {
        ...(existing?.meta ?? {}),
        ...config.meta,
      },
      source: "build",
      isReadOnly: true,
    });
  }
```

API first, then build-time overwrites the same slug.

---

## Bullet by bullet

| Doc | Code | Verdict |
|---|---|---|
| API-only stay `source: "api"` and editable | API put in the map, never visited by the second loop → `source: "api"`, `isReadOnly: false` | **Right** |
| Build-time always `source: "build"` and `isReadOnly: true` | Second loop always sets those, including when it overwrites an API row | **Right** |
| Same slug → one entry | `Map` keyed by `slug` | **Right** |
| Overlapping **meta** keys: build wins | `{ ...existing.meta, ...config.meta }` — later spread wins | **Right** |
| API-only meta keys kept | `existing.meta` spread first, so keys not in build meta remain | **Right** |

Example, slug `care_kutumba_fe`:

```
API:   { url: "https://…",  extra: "from-db" }
Build: { name: "kutumba",   url: "http://localhost:…" }

merged.meta = { extra: "from-db", name: "kutumba", url: "http://localhost:…" }
merged.source = "build"
merged.isReadOnly = true
```

`url` from build wins. `extra` from API is kept.

---

## Not wrong, but incomplete

Docs talk about API vs build. Build-time itself is two lists: `careConfig.careApps` then `localDevPluginConfigs`. **Same slug:** local `apps/` entry **replaces** the env entry (`configs.set` in `getBuildTimePlugConfigs`). The merge section does not mention that.

Sort: merged list puts `source: "build"` before `source: "api"`, then slug. Not in these bullets; still true.

`isReadOnly` is set in merge; this architecture note’s later “admin UI” claims are a separate check (`PlugConfigList` today lists the API response only). For **lines 24–30**, the merge code matches.

After two plugins have loaded, it looks like this:

```ts
window.__CARE_PLUGIN_RUNTIME__ = {
  meta: {
    care_abdm_fe: {
      name: "care_abdm_fe",
      url: "https://ohcnetwork.github.io/care_abdm_fe/",
      package: "ohcnetwork/care_abdm_fe",
      config: { faceAuthUrl: "https://…" },
    },
    care_kutumba_fe: {
      name: "care_kutumba_fe",
      localPath: "/local-plugins/care_kutumba_fe",
      package: "local/care_kutumba_fe",
    },
  },
};
```

Read one plugin’s extra config:

```ts
window.__CARE_PLUGIN_RUNTIME__.meta.care_abdm_fe.config.faceAuthUrl
```

`meta` is **`{ [slug]: PlugConfigMeta }`**, not a single plugin object. Keys are slugs (`care_abdm_fe`), values are that plugin’s merged `config.meta` (frozen). Local `apps/` entries often have `localPath` and no `url`; remotes have `url`.

**What CodeRabbit is talking about**

Two files disagree about the **shape** of `window.__CARE_PLUGIN_RUNTIME__.meta`.

**Runtime** (`PluginEngine`) — a **map** by plugin slug:

```103:116:src/PluginEngine.tsx
  const pluginMeta = useMemo(() => {
    return pluginsQuery.reduce(
      (acc, plugin) => {
        if (!plugin.isLoading && plugin.meta) {
          acc[plugin.slug] = deepFreeze({ ...plugin.meta });
        }
        return acc;
      },
      {} as Record<string, PlugConfigMeta>,
    );
  }, [pluginsQuery]);

  useEffect(() => {
    window.__CARE_PLUGIN_RUNTIME__ = deepFreeze({ meta: pluginMeta });
```

So: `meta.care_abdm_fe`, `meta.care_hello_fe`, …

**Type** (`index.tsx`) — one blob:

```21:21:src/index.tsx
    __CARE_PLUGIN_RUNTIME__: { meta: PlugConfigMeta };
```

That says `meta` **is** a `PlugConfigMeta` (`url`, `name`, `config`), not a dictionary of them.

The **doc** already tells the truth: runtime is the slug map; `Window` is wrong.

Tiny picture:

```
Type says:   runtime.meta.url
Reality:     runtime.meta.care_abdm_fe.url
```

---

**Is the review valid?**

**Yes**, as a **code** type-contract bug. Plugins cannot import host modules, so they rely on `Window`. If they follow `index.tsx`, they read the wrong shape.

It is **not** “docs don’t match runtime.” Docs match runtime. CodeRabbit’s “align the documented type with `index.tsx`” is easy to read backwards: **do not** change the doc to the wrong type. **Change `index.tsx` to the runtime shape.**

“Major / data integrity” is heavy for this. Nothing is corrupted. Plugin TS is misleading. Also TS may **not** error today: `PlugConfigMeta` has `[key: string]: unknown`, so a slug map can still be assigned to it.

---

**Necessary?**

- **In this docs PR:** no. Line 91 already records the mismatch. You can merge docs without fixing `Window`.
- **For the host contract:** yes, eventually. One-line type fix. Don’t need a new “compatibility issue” tracker if you just patch `index.tsx`.

---

**The fix** (code, not the architecture note):

```ts
__CARE_PLUGIN_RUNTIME__: { meta: Record<string, PlugConfigMeta> };
```

Optional: type the `PluginEngine` assignment so it can’t drift again.

Then drop the last sentence of doc line 91 (“The `Window` type currently…”).

I would **not** run CodeRabbit’s `rg` as a merge gate; we already grepped: only `index.tsx` (type), `PluginEngine` (write), and this doc.

**Takeaway:** comment is valid about the **type**. Fix `Window` in `index.tsx`. Don’t rewrite the doc to match the bug. Say if you want that one-line type fix in this PR.

You don’t need a Kutumba feature for this. The bug is the **host type vs live object**. Kutumba is just the plugin you already load.

Load CARE with Kutumba on (either `apps/care_kutumba_fe` + `npm run dev`, or env + preview). Open a page where the plugin actually ran (so `PluginEngine` has written the global).

